A||||13||Shiner||3.2 V6 questions||||5
Z||000000||Shiner||10-31-1999||01:05 AM||||Hey guys.  In the future, I will be doing some kind of engine work on my car, be it a swap or a rebuild of my 2.8.  I was thinking if you rebuilt the 2.8 real hot, with a 3.1 crank, and bored over 1mm (assume I use ALL the hot shit, headers, hot cam...etc).  What kind of HP and TQ am I looking at here?  Will it be reliable?  Then assume I slap on at least a 70hp shot of nitro (!) for those encounters with corvettes and whatnot.  Is this a good idea?  What kind of quarters would I be running (5speed getrag and a pretty good driver)?  Would the nitro wreck this engine quickly, or will it hold up (assume I use forged pistons and rods)  Let me know.  I really want to hear from people with expierence in actually doing this setup.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Eric||10-31-1999||11:35 AM||||Be prepared to spend some serious money on forged pistons.  You'll have to use JE pistons and those will run in the $600-1000 range.  The original rods are forged and should work fine as long as you don't get too crazy with the laughing gas.  Get a good machinist who understands and is willing to work on the 60 degree V6 motor.  And get a Chevy Power book.  It explains what you can do with this motor to make some serious horsepower.  One cool trick is to put small block Chevy rocker arm studs in the head and use a narrow roller rocker arm with a 1.6 ratio.  You'll need a good crank like the nodular steel one that GM Performance makes $375.  Maybe a roller cam if you can find one that will work.  And get the whole damn engine balanced!  Everthing needs to be perfect if you plan on squeezing every pony out of this engine.  If everything is built right and you don't use more than a 70 shot, then there should be no problem with reliability.  You would be looking at a motor without nitrous that makes around 220-250hp and 230ft/lbs of torque if everything is perfect including the computer.  That will put you in the low 14s in the quarter and who knows with the nitrous.  I'll be running the economy version with 200hp and 220 ft/lbs of torque.  Should put my 88GT deep in the 14s.||||reg||1
Z||000002||WKDFIRO||11-05-1999||08:48 PM||||Unfortunately from the 3.2s that I have driven and the numbers that we at Fiero Motorsports have seen, 3.2 will see the high side of 190 and no more. We even shaved the deck height to get more compression. The way to go is 3.4 out of the 91-95 Camaro/Firebird/S10. Its rating STARTS at 160 hp. Not impressed? The torque rating is at 200 before we play with the cam, roller rockers, headers and heads. These engines accept all of the intake and exhaust of the 2.8 which in California is almost a must when we visit Mr. Smogman. The yard has a lot of old 2.8s if you still want to rebuild...||||reg||1
Z||000003||Shiner||11-06-1999||12:05 AM||||You know, you are right.  If I dropped in the 3.4, which would be pretty easy, as engine swaps go, I would have much more HP potential.  I would consider the 3.4 twin cam, but I really like torque.  To me, torque is more fun (burnouts, hanging the tail around turns..) then high end HP.  Soooo, let's say I take the 3.4 and do all that same stuff to it?  What kind of HP and TQ ratings would I be looking at there??||||reg||6
Z||000004||jhogans||11-06-1999||01:03 AM||||Ed Parks from the Fiero Factory told me that you would have the following with a 3.4 engine swap.<P>w/out headers 175 hp<BR>w/headers 185-195 hp<BR>w/turbo 240-250 hp.||||reg||1
Z||000005||mwbackus||11-06-1999||01:12 AM||||You can get a "brand new" S-10 conversion HT3.4 shortblock for less than $1700. <A HREF="http://www.sallee-chevrolet.com/PriceList/GmPerformanceEngines.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.sallee-chevrolet.com/PriceList/GmPerformanceEngines.html</A>  <P>Horsepower 160 @ 5,000 RPM, Torque 194 Ft.lbs. @ 2700 RPM<P>It uses the #12363220 cam: <BR>.427" Int., .454" Exh. Lift / 204° Int., 216° Exh Duration<P>1.6:1 ratio rockers are available from both Comp Cams and Crane made specifically for the 60° HO heads 10mm studs.  The use of these higher ratio rockers with the above cam would yield a .456" Int., .484" Exh Lift.  <BR>Not exactly "stock computer friendly", but it sure would be fun.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000006||superXmotorsports||11-06-1999||02:16 AM||||Shiner - I just bought a 3.2 from someone who had it professionally built for autocrossing: 10.5 Venolia forged pistons, Crane cam, full length headers, Corvette TPI w/ superchips custom CPU dyno'd at 255hp.  My last GT I just sold had a stroker that punched out 210 (+/-) and ran 14.4 in the quarter.||||reg||6
Z||000007||Shiner||11-06-1999||02:25 AM||||Thanks for the replies, keep em rollin in  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>I think WHEN (my 2.8 runs good now) I have the $$, I will go with the 3.4, with ALL the goodies.  If you're gonna do it, you might as well do it big!  Who knows, maybe twin turbos?  It would be a BEOTCH to work on with all that extra plumbing, but a hot-rod 3.4 with twin turbos would sure smoke! Bad, bad choice of words...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000008||batboy||11-06-1999||07:49 AM||||I plugged the following high performance 3.4 engine into my Desktop Dyno program: 3.4 V6, GM HO cam, 9:1 compression ratio, small tube headers, HO heads with pocket porting, etc. This setup makes 220 HP at 5500 RPM. The head porting really helps flow at the upper end and works well with this cam.<P>But, the best thing is that peak torque is 230 ft/lbs at 4000-4500 RPM. The torque numbers stay above 200 ft/lbs all the way from 2000 RPM to 5000 RPM. That's the kind of flat torque curve you want for the stop light grand prix. That's "sit you back in the seat" mid-range power.||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||11-06-1999||08:24 AM||||I took another look at the numbers from that 3.4 crate engine. Remember, GM is known to under-rate the power numbers of their engines. Anyway, I plugged the crate engine (listed in a previous post) into my Desktop Dyno and got 170 HP at 5000 RPM and 200 ft/lbs of torque at 3500 RPM.<P>Pretty close, the computer is slightly more optimistic on the HP than GM claims. Take this same engine, add headers and pocket port the heads, then you have the engine that I described in my last post. Not enough power? How about a 70 shot NOS system or a turbo? Suddenly, 300 horsepower don't seem so far away after all.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Raydar||11-06-1999||09:01 AM||||When GM published the HP specs (160), was it likely that they were basing that figure on an S-10 installation? A Fiero has about 10'-15' less pipe to restrict the exhaust. All the info that I've seen on "FWD into Fiero" swaps (3800's, Quad 4's) seems to suggest about a 15 HP increase, doing nothing else. Just my $.02. (Of course, if GM's figures were measured on a dyno, at the crankshaft, then I'm just wasting time and bandwidth. In that case, Nevermind.)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||1
